---
import "../sternzeit/sternzeit.css";
import "./dunstkreis.css";

const TABLES = [
    { name: "transmittance", title: "Transmittance", axes: "view angle × altitude" },
    { name: "multiScattering", title: "Multiple scattering", axes: "sun angle × altitude" },
    { name: "skyView", title: "Sky view", axes: "azimuth from the sun × angle from the horizon" },
];
---

<figure class="scene-frame wide" id="lookup">
    <div class="dome-scene lookup-scene">
        <svg class="dome-layer" data-field="scene" aria-label="A view ray through the air, its samples and their lookups"></svg>
        <div class="dome-compass" data-field="compass" aria-hidden="true"></div>
        <div class="lookup-panel">
            {
                TABLES.map((table) => (
                    <div class="lookup-sub">
                        <div class="lookup-table lut-stage" data-lut={table.name}>
                            <canvas data-field="image" aria-label={`The ${table.title.toLowerCase()} table`} />
                            <canvas data-field="overlay" aria-hidden="true" />
                        </div>
                        <span class="lookup-caption">
                            <span class="subfigure-title">{table.title.toLowerCase()}</span>: {table.axes}
                        </span>
                    </div>
                ))
            }
        </div>
        <p class="note lookup-info" data-field="info"></p>
        <p class="vis-credit footnote">
            Drag to rotate. Visualized with <a href="https://zzz.dog/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Zdog</a>.
        </p>
    </div>
    <p class="lut-controls lookup-controls">
        <label title="Integration samples along each of the sky view's view rays, dunstkreis' own setting: changing it here recomputes the sky view, as in the tables figure below">
            samples per view ray
            <input type="range" data-field="samples" min="1" max="128" step="1" />
            <output data-field="samplesValue"></output>
        </label>
    </p>
    <figcaption class="figure-caption">
        One pixel of sky, traced back: the view ray from the eye (white), its samples, and at each the two lookups it
        was built from, the transmittance towards the Sun (blue) and the multiple scattering (orange). The Sun is a
        direction, not a place: its light arrives in parallel. (c) The sky-view table keeps what the whole ray
        gathered, in one texel, read with (a) the transmittance and (b) the multiple scattering at every sample. The
        texels the kept ray reads are marked solid, the one under the pointer dashed; point at a texel to see what it
        stands for, click to keep it. A schematic: the air drawn far thicker than it is, the planet curving one and a
        half times as fast, every ray straight and three times as steep; the marks in the tables come from the true
        ray, bent by the air.
    </figcaption>
</figure>

<script>
    import "./lookup.js";
</script>
